{{/* Emits require_sudo — the sudo probe and defer contract that every root-touching WSL
script repeated verbatim, differing only in the noun. Callers set TAG, include this, then
call `require_sudo "<what is being deferred>"`.

Its own template rather than a helper inside linux-install.sh: a script that needs the probe
and nothing else would otherwise pull in a 383-line module, along with its mkdir and package-
manager detection side effects, to save four lines. is-wsl and is-desktop-linux exist for the
same reason — borrow one behavior without importing a module. Scripts that already include
linux-install.sh can include both; have_sudo is itself just `sudo -v` and does not conflict.

Named without an extension like the other micro-templates here, not least because the
lint-after-edit hook shellchecks any *.sh and this file is a Go template, not shell.

It exits rather than returns, unlike everything in linux-install.sh: every caller is a
run_once_ script whose contract is to fail loud and retry on the next apply rather than
half-apply without root. */ -}}
require_sudo() {  # $1 = what is being deferred, for the message
  sudo -v && return 0
  # Defaulted rather than bare $1: callers run under `set -u`, and this line is reached only
  # on the sudo-less path, so a caller that forgot the argument would trade its defer message
  # for an "unbound variable" exactly when the message is the whole point.
  echo "$TAG: sudo unavailable; deferring ${1:-this change} (retries next apply)" >&2
  exit 1
}
